# .NET service Vertical Slice layout

Use this shape for Identity, Customers, Tickets, and new .NET APIs.

## Target layout

```
src/services/{name}/
├── Crm.{Name}.Api/
│   ├── Program.cs              # composition only
│   ├── DependencyInjection.cs  # services + MapEndpoints
│   ├── Features/
│   │   └── {Area}/
│   │       └── {UseCase}/
│   │           ├── Endpoint.cs
│   │           ├── Command.cs | Query.cs
│   │           ├── Handler.cs
│   │           ├── Validator.cs   # when input needs checks
│   │           └── Response.cs    # when not using shared contracts DTO
│   ├── Domain/                 # or Crm.{Name}.Domain project
│   └── Infrastructure/         # or Crm.{Name}.Infrastructure project
```

When a service grows, split `Domain/` and `Infrastructure/` into sibling projects referenced by the Api host.

## Rules

1. **Program.cs** — create builder, call `Add{Name}Api()`, build app, call `Use{Name}Api()` / `MapEndpoints()`, `Run()`. No route lambdas in Program.
2. **Each use case** owns its HTTP mapping in `Endpoint.cs` (`IEndpoint.MapEndpoint`).
3. **Do not** put multiple unrelated endpoints in one file; one use case folder = one HTTP surface (or a tightly related pair).
4. Keep MediatR `Command`/`Query` + `Handler` in the same use-case folder.
5. Prefer shared DTOs in `contracts/csharp/Crm.Contracts`; put slice-local result types in `Response.cs`.
6. Nest under an **Area** (`Users`, `Auth`, `Tickets`, …) then the use case (`CreateUser`, `IssueToken`, …).

## Anti-patterns

- Fat `Program.cs` with all `MapGet`/`MapPost` handlers
- Flat `Features/CreateUser/CreateUserCommand.cs` mixing command + handler + endpoint
- Cross-slice static helpers on Handlers — put shared issuance/persistence in Infrastructure
